00:32:10.670 --> 00:32:12.000
applications.

00:32:12.000 --> 00:32:14.090
And I think if you go to,
really, any engineering

00:32:14.090 --> 00:32:16.298
department, they're going
to say, yeah, we have APIs,

00:32:16.298 --> 00:32:20.060
and, yeah, we develop new
APIs, and that's great.

00:32:20.060 --> 00:32:24.890
But when you begin to focus on
a different perspective of that,

00:32:24.890 --> 00:32:28.820
when you start looking from
this outside-in perspective

00:32:28.820 --> 00:32:31.880
and saying, what do
our customers want?

00:32:31.880 --> 00:32:33.980
And then I'm going
to build an API that

00:32:33.980 --> 00:32:36.950
delivers what they want.

00:32:36.950 --> 00:32:40.040
And I'm only going to
connect that to the back end

00:32:40.040 --> 00:32:41.870
when it makes sense.

00:32:41.870 --> 00:32:46.040
Now, you're starting
to shift that mindset.

00:32:46.040 --> 00:32:47.790
And it is hard.

00:32:47.790 --> 00:32:52.340
It's very hard to
completely change

00:32:52.340 --> 00:32:56.900
how engineers look at APIs.

00:32:56.900 --> 00:33:02.360
But once again, I think the
context of this entire webcast

00:33:02.360 --> 00:33:06.230
was to paint APIs in a different
way so that you can take that,

00:33:06.230 --> 00:33:09.710
and you can go off, and you can
say, look, we want to innovate,

00:33:09.710 --> 00:33:15.050
and we want to look at
APIs differently so that we

00:33:15.050 --> 00:33:18.050
can achieve these really
interesting and exciting things

00:33:18.050 --> 00:33:20.960
that we know our
customers are asking for.

00:33:20.960 --> 00:33:21.740
That's the key.

00:33:21.740 --> 00:33:25.040
And maybe it starts by
actually having a meeting where

00:33:25.040 --> 00:33:28.490
you bring together your business
teams and your technology

00:33:28.490 --> 00:33:32.180
teams, and you outline
what that vision is.

00:33:32.180 --> 00:33:37.130
And as you come together
as a cohesive view of APIs,

00:33:37.130 --> 00:33:39.470
then I think you're going
to be able to get to where

00:33:39.470 --> 00:33:45.140
Pitney Bowes is at, OK?

00:33:45.140 --> 00:33:48.140
Let's see, we'll get
one more in here.

00:33:48.140 --> 00:33:51.650
Do you see APIs being
used differently

00:33:51.650 --> 00:33:54.360
in different industries?

00:33:54.360 --> 00:33:55.216
No.

00:33:55.216 --> 00:34:00.180
[LAUGHS] Yeah, it's an
interesting question.

00:34:00.180 --> 00:34:04.230
But the reality is, I think
at the end of the day,

00:34:04.230 --> 00:34:07.420
I share with people and
I share with customers

00:34:07.420 --> 00:34:10.350
this simple idea.

00:34:10.350 --> 00:34:13.190
Do you have customers?

00:34:13.190 --> 00:34:18.770
I think in every single
situation, whether you're B2C,

00:34:18.770 --> 00:34:21.020
or even if you're B2B--

00:34:21.020 --> 00:34:24.350
those B2B situations,
the other businesses

00:34:24.350 --> 00:34:27.080
are still your customers--

00:34:27.080 --> 00:34:29.030
everybody has
customers, and everybody

00:34:29.030 --> 00:34:33.659
is trying to meet the
demands of those customers.

00:34:33.659 --> 00:34:36.380
So they're all using
APIs in a way that

00:34:36.380 --> 00:34:40.040
allows them to accelerate
their development,

00:34:40.040 --> 00:34:43.122
build new things, again,
reach markets faster, all

00:34:43.122 --> 00:34:45.080
of these things we've
been talking about today.

00:34:45.080 --> 00:34:47.300
But ultimately, it's
in a way that you can

00:34:47.300 --> 00:34:50.040
meet the demands of customers.

00:34:50.040 --> 00:34:53.580
And since everybody
has customers,

00:34:53.580 --> 00:34:55.440
all of the industries
are basically

00:34:55.440 --> 00:34:59.430
just applying the
API philosophies

00:34:59.430 --> 00:35:02.850
to meet the needs of their
customers differently.

00:35:02.850 --> 00:35:06.620
But again, I guess,
at the end of the day,

00:35:06.620 --> 00:35:09.350
it's really not that different.

00:35:09.350 --> 00:35:13.240
I want to develop something,
and I want to research it,

00:35:13.240 --> 00:35:15.100
and I want to give
it to my customers,

00:35:15.100 --> 00:35:17.650
and I want to analyze it.

00:35:17.650 --> 00:35:19.390
I have KPIs.

00:35:19.390 --> 00:35:20.585
I have internal goals.

00:35:20.585 --> 00:35:21.710
I want to meet those goals.

00:35:21.710 --> 00:35:23.890
I want to grow my
business, right?

00:35:23.890 --> 00:35:26.930
I think it doesn't really
matter what industry you're in.

00:35:26.930 --> 00:35:30.100
You're all going to be trying
to accomplish the same thing,

00:35:30.100 --> 00:35:32.890
and you're all looking
for ways to innovate.

00:35:32.890 --> 00:35:36.640
And as I've said, APIs
bring a lot of value

00:35:36.640 --> 00:35:39.260
to your ability to innovate.

00:35:39.260 --> 00:35:42.010
And I think that's
a great way to end.

00:35:42.010 --> 00:35:44.940
Once again, thank you
all for listening.

00:35:44.940 --> 00:35:46.910
I appreciate the time.

00:35:46.910 --> 00:35:49.190
And as I mentioned, you've
got my email address.

00:35:49.190 --> 00:35:50.920
Once more, I'm Chris Hood.

00:35:50.920 --> 00:35:53.200
I'm with Apigee,
part of Google Cloud.

00:35:53.200 --> 00:35:56.890
You can reach me at
chrishood@google.com.

00:35:56.890 --> 00:35:58.960
Free to email me and
let me know if there's

00:35:58.960 --> 00:36:00.490
anything I can help you with.

00:36:00.490 --> 00:36:01.710
Thank you again.

00:36:01.710 --> 00:36:02.620
Have a great day.

00:36:02.620 --> 00:36:05.970
